{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>But a mist went up from the earth.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): it is the vapor rising from the moistures of the waters in the earth when it is hot, and the watery parts have not yet intermingled with the earthy parts — this is called אד (mist). And because rain comes from this moisture, and the cloud receives it, therefore the cloud too is called in the language of אד, as it is written (Job 36:27): ״יִזְקוּ מָטָר לֵאדוֹ״." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> These are the clouds of glory that would well up and ascend. And according to the Midrash, 'from the earth' refers to the earth mentioned in the first verse, and the welling up of the clouds of glory alludes to the reception of the flow of glory which it receives from the Holy One, blessed be He. And this is what the Rabbis of blessed memory said concerning the matter of vegetation: at that hour the Prince of the World opened and said (Psalms 104:31): ״יְהִי כְבוֹד ה׳ לְעוֹלָם יִשְׂמַח ה׳ בְּמַעֲשָׂיו״ — and he said this by way of prayer, in the manner of what is said, יהי ה׳ אלהינו עמנו. And its meaning is יִשְׂמַח — 'may He rejoice,' that is, so that He may rejoice. And understand this." "<b>And watered the whole face of the ground.</b> These are the spheres and everything below them, for they act upon the earth. 'And watered the whole face of the ground' — the word כל (all) alludes to what the Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: from the four winds of the world was its dust gathered."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:09:16.543884580Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}